</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets:</span> A commonly used dataset in this field is OpinionQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santurkar et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which is based on data from the American Trends Panel Survey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">pew (2014)</a></cite>. However, the scope of OpinionQA is limited in terms of the domains and regions it covers. To address this limitation, we adopt the same workflow used in OpinionQA but expand the range by extracting questions from five representative survey datasets that cover diverse regions, languages and domains: ESS11, ESS9 (European Social Survey) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">ess (2024)</a></cite>, CGSS (Chinese General Social Survey) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">cgs (2003+)</a></cite>, WVS (World Values Survey) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">wvs ()</a></cite>, and CFPS (China Family Panel Studies) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie and Hu (2014)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the CFPS, we selected 10 core questions to be simulated, while for the other datasets, 20 core questions were chosen. For each core question, we included 6–7 background questions that reflect respondents’ characteristics (e.g., gender, age, political orientation). A detailed example of our constructed data sample is provided in the <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We randomly sample 2,000 or 4,000 entries from the dataset as the training set to simulate the process of conducting a real survey. And the overall distribution of the dataset is regarded as the ground-truth distribution to be simulated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompts:</span> We use the original survey questions and responses as prompts. In addition, we append an instruction at the end of each prompt, asking the LLM to directly output its choice, which constrains the LLM to generate only the tokens corresponding to the predefined options.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs:</span> We employ two variants of the Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite> for our experiments: Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-32B. Qwen are open-source LLMs widely applied in various research areas, and are trained on large-scale corpora that include human preference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics:</span> Some previous studies use one-hot accuracy as a metric, which is unsuitable for evaluating simulated distributions. We instead use Kullback-Leibler Divergence (KLD) and Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD): KL measures the information loss when approximating the true distribution, while JSD provides a symmetric and more stable evaluation. All experimental results are averaged over ten random runs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Set as Result (TS):</span> One of the key baselines is directly using the distribution of training set as the predicted result. This baseline is often ignored in previous research, but it provides a simple yet effective benchmark to assess the performance of fine-tuning methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-shot Methods:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct:</span> Directly prompting the LLM to generate the results without any fine-tuning or prompt engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santurkar et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Engineering (PE):</span> Using a designed prompt to better simulate the respondents’ personality and preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-tuning Methods:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AAE:</span> AAE fine-tunes a lightweight conditional probability model that maps the LLM’s output distribution to the distribution of the training set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025c)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token-Level Fine-tuning (TKFT):</span> TKFT directly fine-tunes the token-level output probabilities of the LLM to align with the training set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suh et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Distribution Simulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.T1" title="In 2.4 Fine-Tuning Method ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the accuracy of different methods in simulating true distributions across all datasets. DSA consistently achieves the best performance across all five datasets and both backbone LLMs, clearly outperforming all zero-shot and fine-tuning baselines. Zero-shot approaches exhibit strong distributional bias, showing that directly prompting the LLM without adaptation is inadequate for modeling human choice. Although fine-tuning-based baselines (AAE and TKFT) provide better approximations to the true distributions, their outputs remain close to or only slightly better than the training set baseline. In contrast, DSA’s distribution shift alignment fine-tuning enables it to generate distributions even closer to the true distribution, validating its advantage in estimation of real distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-32B further highlights the role of model capacity. The larger model shows stronger base performance and achieves lower divergence after fine-tuning, but this advantage does not extend to zero-shot settings, where it sometimes performs worse than the smaller model. This demonstrates that scaling alone cannot ensure accurate preference simulation—effective fine-tuning is essential for aligning LLM outputs with human-like choice behavior. Overall, DSA effectively combines model capacity and structured fine-tuning to achieve superior, robust performance across datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.21977v1/data4b.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/264;" width="467" height="264" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.21977v1/data32b.png" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/264;" width="467" height="264" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Comparison of required real survey data to reach a similar KL divergence across different methods, reflecting the relative data efficiency of different approaches.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Data Efficiency and Cost Reduction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An important indicator of whether a method can effectively simulate real survey data is how much survey data it can save, thereby reducing the cost of conducting surveys. This comparison is often ignored in previous studies. To evaluate this aspect, we compare the data efficiency of different methods by estimating how much training data can be saved while maintaining a similar accuracy. Specifically, we measure how many training samples different fine-tuning methods would need to reach similar performance, as shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.3 Distribution Simulation ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While AAE and TKFT achieve certain improvements, their data efficiency gains are limited under our experimental scale. In contrast, DSA consistently reaches comparable accuracy while saving at least 53.48% of the training data, even with an LLM with only 4B parameters. This advantage stems from the second-stage alignment of distribution shifts across backgrounds, which allows the model to generalize learned relations beyond observed samples and thus achieve more accurate distribution than the training set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case1.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case2.png" id="S3.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case3.png" id="S3.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case4.png" id="S3.F4.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case5.png" id="S3.F4.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case6.png" id="S3.F4.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case7.png" id="S3.F4.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig8" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21977v1/case8.png" id="S3.F4.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>JSD of different methods across backgrounds in ESS11. Backgrounds are sorted from left to right by the number of respondents, with rarer backgrounds appearing on the left.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Generalization across Different Backgrounds</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world surveys, data from certain background groups are often difficult to obtain, leading to missing populations in the training data. In some cases of our experiments, some backgrounds are even entirely absent from the training set, referred to as unseen backgrounds. To evaluate how well different methods generalize to these unseen backgrounds, we conduct experiments on two cases from the ESS11 dataset that include such unseen backgrounds. Specifically, we sort all backgrounds by the number of respondents and compute the JSD of each background.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the <a href="#S3.F4" title="In 3.4 Data Efficiency and Cost Reduction ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, DSA significantly reduces the JSD for backgrounds that are difficult to obtain in real surveys, demonstrating its strong generalization capability. Even for unseen backgrounds that not appear in the training set, DSA accurately predicts their choice distributions. This improvement arises from the second-stage distribution shift alignment, by learning how distributions shift rather than fitting observed samples, DSA generalizes these relations to new subgroups.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:86.7pt;vertical-align:-40.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(26.8pt,-6.7pt) scale(1.18376259407962,1.18376259407962) ;">
<table id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
