<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Proportion of backgrounds where each method produces a distribution closer to the true one than the training set.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Robustness across Questions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When simulating survey data, robustness across different questions is crucial, as the true underlying distribution in reality is unobservable. Assuming we have a small amount of real survey data and use a specific method to simulate additional data, we cannot directly verify whether this method actually brings the simulated distribution closer to the true underlying distribution. In addition to measuring absolute accuracy, we also evaluate the relative improvement of each method. Specifically, we calculate the proportion of background groups for which a method produces a distribution closer to the true one than the training set itself. This metric reflects how reliably each method improves upon the observed data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3.5 Generalization across Different Backgrounds ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the proposed DSA achieves improvements on more than 90% of backgrounds across all datasets, indicating that it is effective in most scenarios and carries a very low risk of performance degradation when applied to real survey data. In contrast, the zero-shot methods provide improvements on only around 10% of backgrounds, suggesting that LLM cannot be reliably applied to real survey without fine-tuning. The other two fine-tuning methods, AAE and TKFT, achieve moderate improvements, with the best reaching 63.27%. These results demonstrate that DSA not only enhances overall accuracy but also maintains strong robustness across questions, consistently improving upon the training data in nearly all background groups.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21977v1/trainsetsize.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/230;" width="429" height="230"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>KL divergence of different fine-tuning methods across different training set sizes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Robustness across Training Set Size</h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world applications, surveys vary greatly in scale, and the amount of available training data can differ significantly. Therefore, it is also important whether a fine-tuning method can consistently improve performance across different training set sizes. In this experiment, we evaluate various methods by gradually increasing the amount of real survey data used for fine-tuning and measuring their performance under each setting. As shown in <a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3.6 Robustness across Questions ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, DSA provides most stable and consistent improvements across most of the training set sizes. However, when the training set becomes sufficiently large, the improvements brought by different methods become marginal, suggesting that the primary advantage of LLM-based survey simulation lies in medium-scale surveys, where real survey data are limited but not extremely scarce.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:105.6pt;vertical-align:-49.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(53.0pt,-16.2pt) scale(1.44342838756796,1.44342838756796) ;">
<table id="S3.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
