<span class="ltx_bibblock">No preference left behind: Group distributional preference optimization.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib23.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.20299</em>.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1 (Independence of Background Questions):</span>
The distributions of background questions are independent with each other:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(C\mid b_{1}j_{1},b_{2}j_{2}\dots)\\
=P(C\mid b_{1}j_{1})\cdot P(C\mid b_{2}j_{2})\dots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(C\mid b_{1}j_{1},b_{2}j_{2}\dots)\\
=P(C\mid b_{1}j_{1})\cdot P(C\mid b_{2}j_{2})\dots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the independence assumption, we aim to prove that estimating the distribution using the shifts is an unbiased estimate of the true distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1 (Unbiased Estimation):</span>
Under Assumption 1, using distribution shifts to estimate the choice distribution for a specific background in an unbiased estimate of the true distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof:</span>
Assume we have the distributions for four backgrounds, denoted as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{b}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{b}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[b_{11},b_{21},\dots],\mathbf{b}_{2}=[b_{12},b_{21},\dots]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[b_{11},b_{21},\dots],\mathbf{b}_{2}=[b_{12},b_{21},\dots],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{b}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{b}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[b_{11},b_{22},\dots],\mathbf{b}_{4}=[b_{12},b_{22},\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[b_{11},b_{22},\dots],\mathbf{b}_{4}=[b_{12},b_{22},\dots]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> differ in the first question, and <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> differ in the second question. Since the backgrounds for other questions are the same, we can ignore their influence.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Because the first and second questions are independent, we can express their joint probability as a product of conditional probabilities:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(C\mid b_{11},b_{21})=P(C\mid b_{11})\cdot P(C\mid b_{21})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(C\mid b_{11},b_{21})=P(C\mid b_{11})\cdot P(C\mid b_{21})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">And the conditional probability <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated using the mapping between <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(C\mid b_{11})\cdot P(C\mid b_{21})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(C\mid b_{11})\cdot P(C\mid b_{21})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{12})\cdot P(C\mid b_{21})\cdot P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{12})\cdot P(C\mid b_{21})\cdot P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Then apply this conditional probability to <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{3}=[b_{11},b_{22},\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{3}=[b_{11},b_{22},\dots]</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(C\mid b_{11},b_{22})\cdot P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(C\mid b_{11},b_{22})\cdot P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{11})\cdot P(C\mid b_{22})\cdot P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{11})\cdot P(C\mid b_{22})\cdot P(b_{11}\mid b_{12})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{12})\cdot P(C\mid b_{22})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{12})\cdot P(C\mid b_{22})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{12},b_{22})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(C\mid b_{12},b_{22})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">By applying the shift between <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the unbiased estimate for the distribution of <math id="A1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, this estimation benefits from utilizing all backgrounds that include <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sample size used for the estimation is larger compared to directly using <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> alone, so it can provide a result that is closer to the true distribution than the training set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21977v1/prompt.png" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:452/377;" width="452" height="377" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>An example of constructed sample from ESS.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Prompt</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A1.F6" title="In Appendix A Theoretical Analysis ‣ Distribution Shift Alignment Helps LLMs Simulate Survey Response Distributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> showcases an example of a prompt used in the simulation of survey responses from ESS11. The prompt includes a series of background questions, such as the respondent’s age, gender, location and political questions, designed to capture the respondent’s views on politics. The core question, which is the core question to be simulated, asks the respondent to rate their satisfaction with the German government on a scale from 0 to 10.</p>
</div>
